  "notes": "The duo who standardised sung-verse-into-instrumental-drop pop. The band is the entry's — 95-130 with the crossover core at 100-125 — and the kit is the composite the entry describes: \"four-square kick with clap on 2&4 and 16th hats in the verse; the drop switches to a sparse half-time-feeling kick/snare with the melodic content carrying the energy instead of the percussion\". A single-mode model has to state one kick, so this one states the composite — anchors on 1 and 3 with a 0.28 syncopation and a 2-4 hit density, which reads as four-square when the density lands high and as the sparse drop when it lands low — and that composite is marked INFERRED because the research describes two sections rather than one pattern. The defining structural parameter is authored where it belongs, in the melody: their drops are \"vocalist-driven rather than purely instrumental — the hook is a chopped or sustained guest vocal sitting on a detuned chord stack, which is why their records read as pop songs rather than club tools\". So the melody carries a chopped-vocal timbre and the chords are maj7/add9 loops \"kept static so the topline can carry the song\", at `2_bars_per_chord`. Builds are riser-and-snare-roll. ⚠ The research calls the tempo and drum detail convention-derived; that mark is repeated here, and all velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
